--TEST--
`Core\Config::set` raises a limit as far as the operator's `[limits.hard]` ceiling and refuses above it as `false` with the previous value intact, throwing neither time — `rule:config/three-changeability-classes`, M6's *Verify*
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:config/three-changeability-classes`: `[limits]` states the value a request starts at and `[limits.hard]`
// the ceiling it may raise itself to. `memory` is a bare limit name, which
// `Core\Config` resolves to the `[limits]` entry of the tree below.
echo Core\Config::get('memory'), "\n";

// Under the ceiling the operator kept: accepted, and in force for the rest of
// this request.
try {
    if (Core\Config::set('memory', '256M')) {
        echo "raised to ", Core\Config::get('memory'), "\n";
    } else {
        echo "refused under the ceiling\n";
    }
} catch (Throwable $thrown) {
    echo "threw under the ceiling: ", $thrown->message, "\n";
}

// Above `[limits.hard]`: the refusal is the `false` this member answers with,
// and never an exception. Both halves are the assertion — a version that threw
// would be a different API, and one that accepted would make the ceiling
// advisory.
try {
    if (Core\Config::set('memory', '1G')) {
        echo "raised past the ceiling\n";
    } else {
        echo "refused above the ceiling\n";
    }
} catch (Throwable $thrown) {
    echo "threw above the ceiling: ", $thrown->message, "\n";
}

// The previous value intact: what the refused set left in force is the value
// the accepted one wrote, not the file's and not the one it asked for.
echo Core\Config::get('memory'), "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# The root file, `rule:config/the-root-is-config-else-nvs-toml-else-the-shipped-defaults` step 2's `./nvs.toml`. A `[limits]` entry and the
# `[limits.hard]` ceiling over it are both needed for the case to have two sides
# to assert: one set lands between them and one above.
[limits]
memory = "128M"

[limits.hard]
memory = "512M"
--EXPECT--
128M
raised to 256M
refused above the ceiling
256M
